(CHAPTER I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE FUNCTIONS OF THE STOCK EXCHANGE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Every now and then some one who has not given much thought to the) Tj T*
(matter asks the questions, ?Of what real use is the Stock Exchange??) Tj T*
(?What does it accomplish?? ?Is it a necessary and useful part of our) Tj T*
(economic life, or is it merely a means of promoting speculation and) Tj T*
(gambling?? These are fair questions, and they are asked in good faith.) Tj T*
(To be sure they have been answered many times by writers on economic) Tj T*
(subjects, but the trouble is that in our hurried American life we do) Tj T*
(not read the economists, preferring to get our impressions from the) Tj T*
(hasty utterances of some one who knows no more about it than we do.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The study of any form of economic development, like the study of) Tj T*
(sciences and philosophies, requires infinite patience. But the ?man) Tj T*
(in the street? is bored to death by such methods; he wants to take a) Tj T*
(short cut to his conclusions; merely tasting the Pierian spring he) Tj T*
