(hurries on to judgments that are superficial, haphazard, and often) Tj T*
(crude and blundering. And yet at bottom this man, a good citizen with) Tj T*
(an open mind, invariably wants the truth. He may be too busy to dig it) Tj T*
(up himself, but he knows it when he sees it, and once he has grasped) Tj T*
(it he has no patience with those who seek to turn him from it. To this) Tj T*
(average man, who holds in his hands the balance of power in America, I) Tj T*
(venture to say something about markets.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first thing a man asks when he wishes to buy is ?the price.? Every) Tj T*
(minute of the day, all over the world, that question is on men?s lips.) Tj T*
(As it is a necessary prelude to all forms of trade, it follows that) Tj T*
(everything that enters into the making of prices becomes at once of) Tj T*
(primary importance. The more scientific the price, and the nicer and) Tj T*
(more accurate the making of it, the better the bargain for both buyer) Tj T*
(and seller and for trade generally, bearing in mind the distinction) Tj T*
(between prices, which are temporary and move rapidly--and values, which) Tj T*
(are intrinsic and move slowly. The price of a thing is what you can) Tj T*
(get for it; the value is its real worth to you, and hence it cannot be) Tj T*
(defined or measured, since a thousand considerations may enter into) Tj T*
(it, such as caprice, sentiment or association. If _real_ values could) Tj T*
(be determined, they would necessarily be identical with prices, but as) Tj T*
(they cannot be ascertained in ordinary commodities of trade, prices) Tj T*
(become the really essential considerations and values the subordinate) Tj T*
(ones. Let us see, then, how prices are made, for this is one of the) Tj T*
(reasons why exchanges exist.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If you want to buy, let us say, a piano, you go to the dealer and ask) Tj T*
(the price, and as he is the only person in the neighborhood who deals) Tj T*
(in pianos, you must either accept his offer or look elsewhere. But) Tj T*
(to look elsewhere takes time and labor; dealers in pianos are widely) Tj T*
(separated; moreover, there is no open competition among them such as) Tj T*
(you would like, and so finally when you have bought you feel perhaps) Tj T*
(you have not secured your money?s worth. You would have secured a much) Tj T*
(better bargain, no doubt, had there been twenty dealers in the room) Tj T*
(competing with each other, and a still better bargain had their number) Tj T*
(been fifty, or a hundred, or two hundred, because that would mean) Tj T*
(competition, and the more competition there is, in close contact and) Tj T*
(governed by rigid business rules, the more certain the approach to a) Tj T*
(perfect price. Everywhere in the world fairs and other gatherings of) Tj T*
(merchants are held at periodic intervals because people demand them) Tj T*
(in their effort to secure proper prices by competitive bidding and) Tj T*
(offering. One of the first travelers to penetrate the heart of Africa) Tj T*
(found among the natives this phenomenon of trade, showing that it is) Tj T*
(instinctive; indeed, it may be traced to the earliest known period) Tj T*
(in the history of any people. If you arise before daybreak in London) Tj T*
(and go to Billingsgate and Covent Garden, or in Paris to the _Halles) Tj T*
(Centrales_--Zola?s ?Ventre de Paris?--you will find there the modern) Tj T*
(type of these markets in their utmost perfection.[1]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This is why Exchanges exist, not only Stock Exchanges, but) Tj T*
(market-places of all kinds: Buyers seek the largest market they can get) Tj T*
(in order to obtain the lowest prices; sellers, in order to obtain the) Tj T*
