(SALES OF QUOTATIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The quotations of exchanges have been judicially determined to be) Tj T*
(their own property, which may be sold under contracts limiting their) Tj T*
(use. In addition to supplying its own members in New York City with) Tj T*
(its quotations, the Stock Exchange sells them to the telegraph) Tj T*
(companies, under contracts restricting the delivery of the service in) Tj T*
(New York City to subscribers approved by a committee of the Exchange;) Tj T*
(the contracts are terminable at its option. This restriction would) Tj T*
(imply a purpose on the part of the Exchange to prevent the use of the) Tj T*
(quotations by bucket-shop keepers. But the contracts are manifestly) Tj T*
(insufficient, in that they fail to cover the use of the service in) Tj T*
(places other than New York City; if corroboration were needed it could) Tj T*
(be found in the fact that the quotations are the basis for bucket-shop) Tj T*
(transactions in other cities. In such effort as has been made to) Tj T*
(control these quotations the Exchange has been hampered to some extent) Tj T*
(by the claim that telegraph companies are common carriers, and that) Tj T*
(as such they must render equal service to all persons offering to pay) Tj T*
(the regular charge therefor. This claim has been made in other States) Tj T*
(as well as in New York, and the telegraph companies have in the past) Tj T*
(invoked it as an excuse for furnishing quotations to people who were) Tj T*
(under suspicion, although it was not possible to prove that they were) Tj T*
(operating bucket-shops. Recent decisions seem to hold that this claim) Tj T*
(is not well-founded. We advise that a law be passed providing that,) Tj T*
(so far as the transmission of continuous quotations is concerned,) Tj T*
(telegraph companies shall not be deemed common carriers, or be) Tj T*
(compelled against their volition to transmit such quotations to any) Tj T*
(person; also a law providing that if a telegraph company has reasonable) Tj T*
(ground for believing that it is supplying quotations to a bucket-shop,) Tj T*
(it be criminally liable equally with the keeper of the bucket-shop.) Tj T*
(Such laws would enable these companies to refuse to furnish quotations) Tj T*
(upon mere suspicion that parties are seeking them for an unlawful) Tj T*
(business, and would compel them to refuse such service wherever there) Tj T*
(was a reasonable ground for believing that a bucket-shop was being) Tj T*
(conducted.) Tj T*
