() Tj T*
(The selling of agricultural products for future delivery has been the) Tj T*
(subject of much controversy in recent years. A measure to prohibit such) Tj T*
(selling, known as the Hatch Anti-Option bill, was debated at great) Tj T*
(length in Congress during the years 1892, 1893, and 1894. Although) Tj T*
(it passed both House and Senate in different forms, it was finally) Tj T*
(abandoned by common consent. As shown hereafter, similar legislation) Tj T*
(in Germany has proved injurious; and when attempted by our States it) Tj T*
(has either resulted detrimentally or been inoperative. The subject was) Tj T*
(exhaustively considered by the Industrial Commission of Congress which) Tj T*
(in 1901 made an elaborate report \(Vol. VI\), showing that selling for) Tj T*
(future delivery, based upon a forecast of future conditions of supply) Tj T*
(and demand, is an indispensable part of the world?s commercial future) Tj T*
(delivery has been the subject of machinery, by which prices are, as far) Tj T*
(as possible, equalized throughout the year to the advantage of both) Tj T*
(producer and consumer. The subject is also treated with clearness and) Tj T*
(impartiality in the Cyclopedia of American Agriculture, in an article) Tj T*
(on ?Speculation and Farm Prices?; where it is shown that since, the) Tj T*
(yearly supply of wheat, for example, matures within a comparatively) Tj T*
(short period of time somebody must handle and store the great bulk of) Tj T*
(it during the interval between production and consumption. Otherwise) Tj T*
(the price will be unduly depressed at the end of one harvest and) Tj T*
(correspondingly advanced before the beginning of another.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
