(and in 1896 enacted a law much more drastic. The landowners,) Tj T*
(constituting the powerful Agrarian party, contended that short) Tj T*
(selling lowered the price of agricultural products, and demanded that) Tj T*
(contracts on the Exchange for the future delivery of wheat and flour be) Tj T*
(prohibited. The Reichstag assented to this demand. It yielded also to) Tj T*
(demands for an abatement of stock speculation, and prohibited trading) Tj T*
(on the Exchange in industrial and mining shares for future delivery.) Tj T*
(It enacted also that every person desiring to carry on speculative) Tj T*
(transactions be required to enter his name in a public register, and) Tj T*
(that speculative trades by persons not so registered should be deemed) Tj T*
(gambling contracts and void. The object of the registry was to deter) Tj T*
(the small speculators from stock gambling and restrict speculation to) Tj T*
(men of capital and character.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The results were quite different from the intention of the legislators.) Tj T*
(Very few persons registered. Men of capital and character declined to) Tj T*
(advertise themselves as speculators. The small fry found no difficulty) Tj T*
(in evading the law. Foreign brokers seeing a new field of activity) Tj T*
(opened to them in Germany, flocked to Berlin and established agencies) Tj T*
(for the purchase and sale of stocks in London, Paris, Amsterdam, and) Tj T*
(New York. Seventy such offices were opened in Berlin within one year) Tj T*
(after the law was passed, and did a flourishing business. German) Tj T*
(capital was thus transferred to foreign markets. The Berlin Exchange) Tj T*
(became insignificant and the financial standing of Germany as a whole) Tj T*
(was impaired.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DETRIMENTAL CONSEQUENCES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This, however, was not the most serious consequence of the new law.) Tj T*
(While bankers and brokers, in order to do any business at all, were) Tj T*
(required to register, their customers were not compelled to do so.) Tj T*
(Consequently the latter could speculate through different brokers on) Tj T*
(both sides of the market, pocketing their profits and welching on their) Tj T*
(losses as gambling contracts. Numerous cases of this kind arose, and in) Tj T*
(some the plea of wagering was entered by men who had previously borne a) Tj T*
(good reputation. They had yielded to the temptation which the new law) Tj T*
(held out to them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another consequence was to turn over to the large banks much of the) Tj T*
(business previously done by independent houses. Persons who desired to) Tj T*
(make speculative investments in home securities applied directly to the) Tj T*
(banks, depositing with them satisfactory security for the purchases. As) Tj T*
(the German banks were largely promoters of new enterprises, they could) Tj T*
(sell the securities to their depositors and finance the enterprises) Tj T*
(with the deposits. This was a profitable and safe business in good) Tj T*
(times, but attended by dangers in periods of stringency, since the) Tj T*
(claims of depositors were payable on demand. Here again the law worked) Tj T*
(grotesquely, since customers whose names were not on the public) Tj T*
(register could, if the speculation turned out badly, reclaim the) Tj T*
(collateral or the cash that they had deposited as security.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
