() Tj T*
(MODIFICATION OF LAW IN 1908) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The evil consequences of the law of 1896 brought about its partial) Tj T*
(repeal in 1908. By a law then passed the government may, in its) Tj T*
(discretion, authorize speculative transactions in industrial and mining) Tj T*
(securities of companies capitalized at not less than $5,000,000; the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange Register was abolished; all persons whose names were in) Tj T*
(the ?Handels-register? \(commercial directory\), and all persons whose) Tj T*
(business was that of dealing in securities, was declared legally bound) Tj T*
(by contracts made by them on the Exchange. It provided that other) Tj T*
(persons were not legally bound by such contracts, but if such persons) Tj T*
(made deposits of cash or collateral security for speculative contracts,) Tj T*
(they could not reclaim them on the plea that the contract was illegal.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In so far as the Reichstag in 1896 had aimed to prevent small) Tj T*
(speculators from wasting their substance on the Exchange, it not only) Tj T*
(failed, but, as we have seen, it added a darker hue to evils previously) Tj T*
(existing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Germany is now seeking to recover the legitimate business thrown away) Tj T*
(twelve years ago. She still prohibits short selling of grain and flour,) Tj T*
(although the effects of the prohibition have been quite different from) Tj T*
(those which its supporters anticipated. As there are no open markets) Tj T*
(for those products, and no continuous quotations, both buyers and) Tj T*
(sellers are at a disadvantage; prices are more fluctuating than they) Tj T*
(were before the passage of the law against short selling.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
